1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,106
Why did the United States buy this

2
00:00:02,106 --> 00:00:03,840
strip of desert from Mexico?

3
00:00:04,190 --> 00:00:07,045
This is the Gadsden Purchase: the southern

4
00:00:07,045 --> 00:00:09,832
part of Arizona and New Mexico, including

5
00:00:09,832 --> 00:00:11,123
the city of Tucson.

6
00:00:11,473 --> 00:00:15,020
The deal was signed in December 1853,

7
00:00:15,020 --> 00:00:18,280
and the US paid Mexico ten million

8
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:19,047
dollars.

9
00:00:19,397 --> 00:00:22,959
For about seventy-seven thousand square kilometers of

10
00:00:22,959 --> 00:00:25,647
land, almost the size of South Carolina.

11
00:00:25,997 --> 00:00:28,336
So why buy a desert? A railroad.

12
00:00:28,336 --> 00:00:30,749
The US wanted a southern route to

13
00:00:30,749 --> 00:00:33,819
California, and this flat land avoided the

14
00:00:33,819 --> 00:00:35,939
high mountains further north.

15
00:00:36,289 --> 00:00:39,918
Mexico's president, Antonio López de Santa Anna,

16
00:00:39,918 --> 00:00:42,565
needed money after years of war, so

17
00:00:42,565 --> 00:00:43,926
he agreed to sell.

18
00:00:44,276 --> 00:00:46,136
It was the last big piece of

19
00:00:46,136 --> 00:00:48,992
land added to the lower forty-eight states,

20
00:00:48,992 --> 00:00:51,251
and it drew most of today's border

21
00:00:51,251 --> 00:00:53,044
between Mexico and Arizona.

22
00:00:53,394 --> 00:00:55,762
And the railroad? It finally crossed the

23
00:00:55,762 --> 00:00:58,366
region about twenty-five years later, in the

24
00:00:58,366 --> 00:00:59,431
eighteen-eighties.

25
00:00:59,781 --> 00:01:01,666
Was it a fair deal for Mexico?

26
00:01:01,666 --> 00:01:03,173
Tell me in the comments.

